WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.467
Two more

00:00:00.467 --> 00:00:01.501
sentences are being

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:02.369
handed down today

00:00:02.369 --> 00:00:04.037
for members of the Oath Keepers,

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:04.537
a far

00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:06.006
right extremist group

00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:06.840
for their role

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.142
in the January six Capitol attack.

00:00:09.142 --> 00:00:10.877
One of them is Army veteran

00:00:10.877 --> 00:00:12.212
Jessica Watkins.

00:00:12.212 --> 00:00:13.413
She was just sentenced to eight

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:15.215
and a half years in prison.

00:00:15.215 --> 00:00:16.549
Sentencing for the other member.

00:00:16.549 --> 00:00:18.018
Kenneth Harrelson is happening

00:00:18.018 --> 00:00:19.552
this very hour.

00:00:19.552 --> 00:00:20.720
This all comes one day

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:21.821
after the group's founder,

00:00:21.821 --> 00:00:23.289
Stuart Rhodes, was hit

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:25.091
with the most severe sentence

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:27.360
yet tied to the Capitol insurrection

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:28.261
with a judge

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:29.095
describing him

00:00:29.095 --> 00:00:31.264
as an ongoing threat to democracy.

00:00:31.631 --> 00:00:32.899
The unrepentant Rhodes

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:35.402
was sentenced to 18 years in prison.

00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:38.071
CNN's Evan Perez is covering this for us.

00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:39.839
We also have former federal prosecutors

00:00:39.839 --> 00:00:40.740
who to break down

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.409
the legal aspects of this.

00:00:42.409 --> 00:00:43.476
Evan, starting with you.

00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:45.812
How does this Watkins sentence measure up

00:00:46.012 --> 00:00:48.014
with what prosecutors were calling for?

00:00:48.014 --> 00:00:48.481
Well,

00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:51.017
the prosecutors were asking for 18 years.

00:00:51.017 --> 00:00:53.686
And but it's very clear that

00:00:54.287 --> 00:00:55.955
certainly the judge believed that

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:56.956
Jessica Watkins

00:00:58.091 --> 00:01:00.260
told the story that, you know,

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:01.027
certainly

00:01:01.027 --> 00:01:02.896
maybe got a little bit of empathy

00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:04.197
from the judge

00:01:04.197 --> 00:01:06.332
because of the fact that, you know,

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:07.700
she's struggled through a lot

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.936
she identifies as a transgender

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:11.471
person and

00:01:11.471 --> 00:01:12.639
served in the military,

00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:15.341
described how difficult that was for her.

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:16.876
And really

00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:19.179
at least in the end finally said that

00:01:19.345 --> 00:01:20.814
that she believed

00:01:20.814 --> 00:01:22.449
she now realizes

00:01:22.449 --> 00:01:23.016
what happened

00:01:23.016 --> 00:01:24.050
that day was wrong

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:25.852
and that she should be punished

00:01:25.852 --> 00:01:26.553
and was willing

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:27.587
to accept that punishment.

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:29.622
The prosecutors were not buying it.

00:01:29.622 --> 00:01:31.891
They're citing some of her communications

00:01:32.692 --> 00:01:34.427
even from jail, where she was making

00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:36.429
fun of the police officers saying,

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:37.130
boo hoo,

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:39.833
and saying that they incited the riot

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:42.602
by attacking innocent protesters.

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:44.304
Of course, that's not what happened.

00:01:44.304 --> 00:01:45.705
So, you know, the judge

00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:46.639
the judge, though,

00:01:46.639 --> 00:01:48.041
did recognize again

00:01:48.041 --> 00:01:48.842
that this is a person

00:01:48.842 --> 00:01:50.944
who has gone through a lot and

00:01:51.077 --> 00:01:52.745
and really called out,

00:01:52.745 --> 00:01:53.246
call out,

00:01:53.246 --> 00:01:55.381
called out her her conduct and said,

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:56.282
you know, this is

00:01:56.282 --> 00:01:57.484
this is somebody who

00:01:57.484 --> 00:01:59.152
went through all of this

00:01:59.152 --> 00:02:00.720
all of this personal struggle

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:01.888
and should have shown more

00:02:01.888 --> 00:02:02.822
empathy to the people

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:04.858
who are working in the capital that day.

00:02:04.858 --> 00:02:05.859
And yet again,

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:06.626
Stewart Rhodes,

00:02:06.626 --> 00:02:08.228
the ringleader of the Oath Keepers,

00:02:08.228 --> 00:02:09.262
he got 18 years.

00:02:09.262 --> 00:02:11.264
Prosecutors wanted 25.

00:02:11.264 --> 00:02:13.533
He didn't actually enter the capital

00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:14.134
on that day.

00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:15.602
And that's part of the argument

00:02:15.602 --> 00:02:17.270
that his lawyers were making.

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:18.271
These other two

00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:19.506
that are being sentenced today,

00:02:19.506 --> 00:02:20.707
including Jessica Watkins.

00:02:20.707 --> 00:02:22.375
She did enter the capital. Right.

00:02:22.375 --> 00:02:23.810
And I think this judge made a

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:24.811
is really showing himself

00:02:24.811 --> 00:02:25.178
to be

00:02:25.178 --> 00:02:27.213
a very discerning

00:02:27.213 --> 00:02:29.115
and compassionate judge.

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:29.549
I think, you know,

00:02:29.549 --> 00:02:30.750
the federal judges often

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.552
don't have as much experience

00:02:32.552 --> 00:02:33.386
sentencing people

00:02:33.386 --> 00:02:35.455
who have committed violent crimes

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:37.023
and he's really taking a careful

00:02:37.023 --> 00:02:38.191
look at these circumstances

00:02:38.191 --> 00:02:39.325
to distinguish.

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:40.593
Now, I was a little bit surprised.

00:02:40.593 --> 00:02:41.427
I thought Rhodes

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:42.595
and this was a little bit late,

00:02:42.595 --> 00:02:43.730
given that the prosecutors

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:45.765
were asking for the 25 years

00:02:45.765 --> 00:02:48.001
and given his very stern rebuke

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:50.303
to Rhodes, saying, you're still a danger.

00:02:50.303 --> 00:02:50.703
And so

00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:52.438
a lot of times with violent criminals,

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:54.541
part of the point of a long sentence

00:02:54.541 --> 00:02:56.543
is having them age out

00:02:56.543 --> 00:02:57.410
so they're less violent

00:02:57.410 --> 00:02:58.578
when they get out.

00:02:58.578 --> 00:03:01.314
But with Rhodes, he's really a leader.

00:03:01.314 --> 00:03:02.882
It's not that he's personally violent.

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:05.051
He's inspiring, he's talking and such.

00:03:05.318 --> 00:03:06.252
And that's the danger.

00:03:06.252 --> 00:03:06.953
And I think the judge

00:03:06.953 --> 00:03:08.421
of probably appropriately

00:03:08.421 --> 00:03:10.123
is recognizing the distinction

00:03:10.123 --> 00:03:12.192
between that kind of a leader,

00:03:12.525 --> 00:03:13.960
mastermind of these things

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:15.662
versus the people who are still more

00:03:15.662 --> 00:03:17.830
the foot soldiers and varying degrees.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:18.097
But it's

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:18.598
clear, though,

00:03:18.598 --> 00:03:19.065
like, you know,

00:03:19.065 --> 00:03:19.933
the judge points out

00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:22.402
that Watkins was not just a foot soldier.

00:03:22.402 --> 00:03:25.004
She was definitely a leader of this Ohio

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:26.506
group of Oath Keepers.

00:03:26.506 --> 00:03:26.973
And, you know,

00:03:26.973 --> 00:03:28.541
she was using encrypted communications.

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:29.943
She was giving instructions

00:03:29.943 --> 00:03:31.477
on how to get rid of evidence.

00:03:32.445 --> 00:03:34.581
Again, very proud of what she was doing.

00:03:34.581 --> 00:03:35.515
That day.

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:37.650
Perhaps now she realizes what she did.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:38.851
And Shane,

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:40.086
you mentioned that part of

00:03:40.086 --> 00:03:42.355
the judge's intent is to keep

00:03:42.355 --> 00:03:44.090
these folks behind bars for long enough.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:44.824
So that they're not threats

00:03:44.824 --> 00:03:45.358
when they come out.

00:03:45.358 --> 00:03:45.625
Part of

00:03:45.625 --> 00:03:47.760
it also has to do with deterrence.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:48.494
Right. Right.

00:03:48.494 --> 00:03:49.562
Yeah, absolutely.

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:50.697
And I think

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:52.131
probably for the deterrence,

00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:53.733
hopefully some people who are

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:55.802
kind of taken up with this kind of notion

00:03:55.802 --> 00:03:56.603
and thinking

00:03:56.603 --> 00:03:58.004
they're seeing this and realizing it's

00:03:58.004 --> 00:03:59.939
not just like a cosplay fantasy

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:00.907
that they can do these things.

00:04:00.907 --> 00:04:02.976
There's no accountability for it.

00:04:02.976 --> 00:04:03.876
And that is certainly

00:04:03.876 --> 00:04:05.378
one of the important points here

00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:05.712
is that

00:04:05.712 --> 00:04:06.879
even though this charge

00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:08.181
has been used very often,

00:04:08.181 --> 00:04:09.782
we haven't seen this, thankfully.

00:04:09.782 --> 00:04:10.450
Very often

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:11.484
it's sending a message out

00:04:11.484 --> 00:04:12.685
that this is real.

00:04:12.685 --> 00:04:13.786
People take this seriously

00:04:13.786 --> 00:04:15.154
people were hurt.

00:04:15.321 --> 00:04:15.555
Yeah.

00:04:15.555 --> 00:04:15.989
I mean, look,

00:04:15.989 --> 00:04:17.257
but one of the things that I think

00:04:17.257 --> 00:04:18.391
is denting that

00:04:18.391 --> 00:04:19.792
the whole idea of trying

00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:21.461
to prevent other people from doing

00:04:21.461 --> 00:04:22.996
this is the fact that you have

00:04:22.996 --> 00:04:24.497
a presidential candidates

00:04:24.497 --> 00:04:26.466
who are out there saying publicly

00:04:26.466 --> 00:04:27.367
that they're planning to

00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:28.668
to pardon some of these people

00:04:28.668 --> 00:04:30.303
because they don't view

00:04:30.303 --> 00:04:31.537
January six in the same way.

00:04:31.537 --> 00:04:33.840
And how do you take that message

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:35.375
that former President Trump

00:04:35.375 --> 00:04:36.509
is speaking of people

00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:36.909
like Stewart

00:04:36.909 --> 00:04:39.112
Rhodes as if they're political prisoners,

00:04:39.112 --> 00:04:40.246
as if he's some kind of martyr?

00:04:40.246 --> 00:04:41.914
Yeah, I think that's very damaging

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:42.582
to the country.

00:04:42.582 --> 00:04:44.217
It does hurt

00:04:44.217 --> 00:04:45.718
the sort of disincentives to people

00:04:45.718 --> 00:04:47.487
because the people most prone

00:04:47.487 --> 00:04:48.855
to falling into this

00:04:48.855 --> 00:04:50.590
are the very people that folks like

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:51.524
Trump is speaking to.

00:04:51.524 --> 00:04:52.992
And that's definitely problematic.

